1. Introduction

In this lab, I continued my previous Laravel 12 project. I added a book registration form to the student portal. Users can enter the book title, author name, and number of pages. After registering a book, it appears in the My Books table on the dashboard.

Each user can see only the books registered under their own account.

2. Files Used

- "app/Models/Book.php"
- "app/Http/Controllers/BookController.php"
- "database/migrations/create_books_table.php"
- "routes/web.php"
- "resources/views/dashboard.blade.php"

3. Database

I created the Book model and migration using:

php artisan make:model Book -m

The books table contains the following fields:

- "id": Primary key
- "user_id": ID of the user who registered the book
- "title": Book title
- "author": Author name
- "pages": Number of pages
- "created_at" and "updated_at": Record timestamps

I used a foreign key for "user_id" to connect each book with a user in the users table.

After editing the migration, I ran:

php artisan migrate

4. Book Registration

I created the "BookController" to handle book registration and display.

The form accepts three inputs: title, author, and pages. Laravel validates the inputs before saving the book. The title can contain up to 150 characters, the author name up to 100 characters, and the number of pages must be between 1 and 10,000.

I used "Auth::id()" to get the ID of the logged-in user and save it with the book.

5. Displaying Books

I updated the dashboard to include a book registration form and a My Books table.

The table displays the book number, title, author, and pages. I used the "index()" method in "BookController" to retrieve the logged-in user's books and show the newest records first.

If the user has not registered any books, the dashboard displays a message saying that no books have been registered yet.

I also added Bootstrap to make the form and table easier to use.

6. Routes

I updated "routes/web.php" and added these routes inside the authentication middleware group:

Route::get('/dashboard', [BookController::class, 'index'])
    ->name('dashboard');

Route::post('/books', [BookController::class, 'store'])
    ->name('books.store');

I replaced the previous dashboard route and kept the existing login and logout routes.

7. Security and Validation

I used the "auth" middleware to protect the dashboard and book registration route.

I also used "@csrf" in the form to protect against forged requests. Laravel validation displays errors when the entered data is incorrect. The previous input is restored using "old()".

The user ID is obtained from the authenticated session instead of accepting it from the form.

